@meta
id: 30-13
title: Read your own diff before anyone else does
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: git-in-vim
par: 64
minutes: 7
vscode: the source control view, clicking the file to see the gutter diff
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
You have been in this file for an hour and you are about to stage it.
One of the changes is the ticket. Two are debris from working it out.

`order.provider.ts.orig` is the committed version of the file, provided
with this lesson — in a repo you would produce it with
`git show HEAD:order.provider.ts > order.provider.ts.orig`, which is all
that vim's own `:DiffOrig` example command does.

Put the two side by side, walk the changes, and take back the two that
should not be in the commit:

  · the `console.log(order)` line
  · the endpoint, which you pointed at `/refunds-test` while checking
    something and never put back

The widened status guard — `'SHIPPED'` and `'DELIVERED'` — is the
ticket. It stays. The `.orig` file is a copy and must not change.

@keys
| `:vert diffsplit file` | open `file` beside this one, both in diff mode |
| `]c` `[c` | next change, previous change |
| `do` | diff obtain — take the other side's version of this hunk |
| `dp` | diff put — push this side's version across |
| `Ctrl-W l` `Ctrl-W h` | to the window on the right, on the left |
| `:diffoff!` | leave diff mode |
| `:!git diff %` | the plain diff of this file, without leaving vim |
| `:r !git log --oneline -5` | read command output straight into the buffer |
| `:!git add %` | stage the file you are editing; `%` is its name |
@hints
`:vert diffsplit order.provider.ts.orig` splits vertically. The new
window opens on the left with the cursor in it, so your first move is
back to the right-hand window.
--
`]c` jumps to the next differing hunk. The first one is the guard —
leave it and press `]c` again.
--
`do` on a hunk that only exists on your side deletes it; on a hunk that
differs, it replaces yours with theirs. Two `do` presses finish this.

@solution
    :vert diffsplit order.provider.ts.orig<Enter>  Ctrl-W l
    ]c  ]c do  ]c do  :wqa<Enter>

`do` and `dp` are the two keys that make this more than a viewer. Both
act on the hunk under the cursor: `do` pulls the other buffer's version
into yours, `dp` pushes yours into the other. One of them is always the
one you want, and which one depends only on which window you are
standing in.

This is the same machinery vim uses as a merge tool, which is the next
lesson but one. It is also the fastest way to answer "what did I
actually change here", because unlike a scrolling diff you can edit
while you read.

The shell is the other half of this. `:!cmd` runs anything without
leaving vim, and `%` expands to the current file name, so `:!git add %`
stages what you are looking at and `:!git diff %` prints the plain diff
to the screen. `:r !git log --oneline -5` goes further and reads the
output *into* the buffer, which is how a commit message ends up
containing the list of commits it covers. None of those run here — the
dojo has no repository of its own to run them against — but they are
three of the four git commands most people ever need from inside vim.

Two habits from this lesson, both of which survive whatever tooling you
end up with. Read your diff before you stage, every time — debug lines
and test endpoints are the two things that most often reach production
in a commit whose message says something else entirely. And when you
find one, fix it where you are rather than making a note to fix it
later.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED' || order.status === 'DELIVERED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    console.log(order);

    await this.http.post('/refunds-test', { orderId: order.id });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

